---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import BranchNameGenerator from '../../components/BranchNameGenerator.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="Git Branch Name Generator" description="Turn a task description into a clean, conventional branch name — type/ticket/description format.">
  <BranchNameGenerator client:load slot="tool" />
  <h2>Why consistent branch naming helps</h2>
  <p>A consistent branch naming convention (type/ticket/description) makes it instantly clear what a branch is for, and plays well with automation that parses branch names for ticket linking or CI rules.</p>

  <h2>Common Branch Naming Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Using vague descriptions</strong> like "fix" or "update" that don't tell teammates anything about the branch's actual purpose weeks later.</li>
    <li><strong>Inconsistent type prefixes</strong> across a team (some using "feature", others "feat"), reducing the convention's usefulness for automation.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use This Branch Name Generator</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Creating a properly formatted branch name for a new ticket</li>
    <li>Standardizing branch naming conventions across a team</li>
    <li>Enabling CI/CD rules that parse branch type from the name</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Branch Naming FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>What's a good branch naming convention?</strong> <code>type/ticket-number/short-description</code> is a common pattern (e.g. <code>feature/JIRA-123/add-login</code>) — it's readable, sortable, and easy for automation to parse for CI rules or ticket linking.</p>
  <p><strong>Should branch names use hyphens or underscores?</strong> Hyphens are the more common convention and match git's own tendency to treat them as word separators in tooling and output, though either works as long as your team is consistent.</p>
</ToolLayout>
